[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Debugging Techniques in Core Java: Breakpoints, Stepping, and Watches"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Debugging is an essential skill for any Java developer. It allows you to identify and fix errors in your code, ensuring that your programs run correctly and efficiently. This tutorial will cover three fundamental debugging techniques: breakpoints, stepping, and watches, using a standard IDE like IntelliJ IDEA or Eclipse."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Setting Up Your Debugging Environment"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before diving into the techniques, ensure you have a suitable IDE installed (IntelliJ IDEA, Eclipse, NetBeans). These IDEs provide built-in debugging tools that make the process significantly easier. Create a simple Java project to practice with."},{"order":5,"type":"heading","text":"Breakpoints"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Breakpoints are markers you set in your code that tell the debugger to pause execution at a specific line. This allows you to inspect the state of your program at that point, such as the values of variables."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting and Removing Breakpoints"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"In most IDEs, you can set a breakpoint by clicking in the gutter (the area to the left of the line numbers) next to the line of code where you want to pause execution. A small icon (usually a red dot) will appear, indicating the breakpoint. To remove a breakpoint, simply click on the icon again."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Example Code with Breakpoint"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"public class BreakpointExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int a = 5;
        int b = 10; // Set a breakpoint here
        int sum = a + b;
        System.out.println("Sum: " + sum);
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, setting a breakpoint on line 4 will pause the program's execution right before the `sum` variable is calculated. You can then inspect the values of `a` and `b`."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Running in Debug Mode"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"To start debugging, you typically need to run your program in 'debug mode'. In IntelliJ IDEA, you can do this by right-clicking in the code editor and selecting 'Debug'. In Eclipse, you can right-click and select 'Debug As' -> 'Java Application'."},{"order":14,"type":"heading","text":"Stepping"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Stepping allows you to execute your code line by line. This is crucial for understanding the flow of execution and identifying where errors occur. There are several stepping commands:"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Step Over:** Executes the current line and moves to the next line in the same method. If the current line is a method call, it executes the entire method without stepping into it."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Step Into:** If the current line is a method call, it steps into the method, allowing you to debug the method's implementation."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Step Out:** Executes the remaining lines in the current method and returns to the calling method."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Example of Stepping"},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"public class SteppingExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int x = 10;
        int y = 5;
        int result = add(x, y); // Step into this method
        System.out.println("Result: " + result);
    }

    public static int add(int a, int b) {
        int sum = a + b;
        return sum;
    }
}"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Set a breakpoint on line 4. When the debugger pauses, use 'Step Into' to enter the `add` method. Use 'Step Over' to execute each line within the `add` method. Use 'Step Out' to return to the `main` method."},{"order":22,"type":"heading","text":"Watches"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"Watches allow you to monitor the values of variables or expressions as your program executes. This is extremely useful for tracking changes and identifying unexpected behavior."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Adding Watches"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"In most IDEs, you can add a watch by right-clicking on a variable name and selecting 'Add to Watches' or a similar option. You can also manually add expressions in the 'Watches' window."},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Example of Watches"},{"order":27,"type":"code","text":"public class WatchExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int count = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            count += i; // Add a watch on 'count'
            System.out.println("Count: " + count);
        }
    }
}"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"Set a breakpoint on line 5. Add `count` to the watches. As you step through the loop, you'll see the value of `count` update in the 'Watches' window."},{"order":29,"type":"subheading","text":"Conditional Breakpoints"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"Conditional breakpoints pause execution only when a specified condition is true. This is useful when you want to stop only under certain circumstances, such as when a variable reaches a specific value."},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting Conditional Breakpoints"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"Right-click on an existing breakpoint and select 'Edit Breakpoint' (or a similar option). You can then enter a condition in the breakpoint settings. For example, you might set a breakpoint to pause only when `count > 5`."},{"order":33,"type":"code","text":"public class ConditionalBreakpointExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int count = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
            count += i;
            System.out.println("Count: " + count);
        }
    }
}"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"Set a breakpoint at line 5 and add the condition `i == 5`. The debugger will only pause when `i` is equal to 5."},{"order":35,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":36,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mastering breakpoints, stepping, and watches is crucial for effective debugging in Java. These techniques allow you to understand the flow of your code, inspect variable values, and identify the root cause of errors. Practice these techniques regularly to become a more proficient Java developer."}]
